### 2. Task outcome (extremely detailed version):
- Protocol applied exactly as written:
  - Population: Adults (≥18) with diagnosed HF.
  - Design: Randomized controlled trials.
  - Comparator: Usual HF care.
  - Intervention: mHealth-delivered education (internet/web programs used on smartphones/tablets or other mobile 
devices, SMS, social-media delivered education; apps included).
- Inclusion decisions (IDs 1, 2, 4, 20):
  - ID 1: RCT; home tablet + scale system included HF information and lifestyle advice on the tablet. The educational 
content was mHealth-delivered; comparator was standard HF information (usual care). Included.
  - ID 2: Three-arm RCT with a website (heartfailurematters.org) intervention versus usual care among stable HF 
outpatients; clear web-based HF education component on top of usual care. Although platform unspecified, typical web 
delivery is mobile-accessible; given conservative stance and direct “website education vs usual care,” Included.
  - ID 4: Randomized clinical trial comparing SMS follow-up (educational/supportive content conveyed via text) vs 
telephone vs regular follow-up in HF patients; SMS arm fits mHealth education via mobile messaging vs routine care. 
Included.
  - ID 20: 2:1 randomized trial testing a mobile phone–assisted case management program with self-care education/support
versus usual care among HF patients. Included as mHealth education delivered via mobile phones.
- Key exclusions and rationale patterns:
  - Not RCTs or non-HF populations or protocols (e.g., feasibility, usability, qualitative, protocols, meta-analyses, 
guidelines): IDs 9, 12–14, 16–19, 29–31, 35–36, 39–41, 42, 44–47, 48–50, 55–57, 60, 62–66, 68–71, 73, 75, 79–81, 83–88, 
